A||||6||GT Bastard||What machine work is really necessary?||||11||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-18-2000||12:18 AM||||I'm getting ready to take my block in to have it hot-tanked, bored, etc... I can see how a person could really get nuts with all the different procedures involved in preparing a block & engine components.  To those of you who have experience with engine work, what machine work is really necessary? To give you a little background, the 2.8 that is being stroked to 3.1 came out of a running Fiero that had bad rings(I know this for a fact.)  The block itself doesn't show any signs of excessive wear at all.  I've got 3.1 hypereutectics pistons, chrome moly rings, and a new 3.1 crank.   Thanks in advance! ||||reg||11
Z||000001||TOM||07-18-2000||02:43 AM||||A couple things I do. I know I will get mixed reviews. I gring all the stress risers and casting flash from the block. And paint the inside with an epoxy paint. Oil wont stick so you get better drain back. I also attatch screens to the valley where the oil drains abck from the heads. This does not apply to all engines. But helps save some money if you have a valve train faILure. as it keeps most of the parts from getting into the pan. <BR>I personnally haVE  had bad luck with moly rings. They wouldn't wear in. blew oil for like 5000 miles till I replaced them with steel.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||07-18-2000||04:01 AM||||I thought moly rings made for a tighter seal?  They blew oil?  Now you're scaring me, man.  Anybody else had problems with moly rings?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-18-2000||08:48 AM||||I agree with Tom as far as removing the slag from the block, chunks of that can break free and get sucked into the oil pump, plus if left (as it ususally is) it can restrict oil return to the pan. If the bosses Tom is referring to are the witness marks left by the chaplets used in casting, I would leave those alone. The area next to them can become porous if ground. And the result is only cosmetic, so no benefit is really gained. I don't know Tom's experience, or what type of prep work he did prior to installing the pistons/rings. But my undertsnding is that if the cylinder isn't honed really well, that moly rings won't seat. I've never used them without honing first, so I can't say I have first hand experience on that. I've used steel rings without honing and never had a problem. <BR>When I have a machine shop do my blocks, I usually have them: <P>* Tank the block<BR>* replace the freeze plugs<BR>* install new cam bearings<BR>* bore/hone the cylinders<P>If necessary, I have them grind the crank, but I've only needed this twice. The rest of the motors I've built I just mic the crank, and if taper and roundness are within spec, I just sand the journals with emery cloth, followed with 1000 using oil as a lubricant. Crank comes out smooth as can be. <P>There are so many new technologies out there, it *IS* easy to get crazy with some of the processes. They have coatings and treatments now. Ceramic coatings for the piston tops, and teflon coating for the side of the piston. Then there are places that do ultrasonic molecular alignement, which is supposed to relieve all the stress in the machined metal. It's crazy.<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||07-18-2000||09:41 AM||||Moly rings do blow oil. Coat the inside of the block with Glyptal similar to epoxy paint just cheaper and what the shop uses. you can get it from Eastwood. I'll send you the address tonite. just make sure if you don't do any crazy work on the crank or anything make sure its balanced! a balanced motor will tach up faster, last longer and will was less HP in vibration and harmonics making you smoother and faster! A windage tray would help. High volume oil pump although not really nessasary since your there might as well.   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-18-2000||05:43 PM||||GT Bastard now I know you are doing a 3.2 rebuild. Because you have a new crankshaft you must have the reciprocating assembly balanced. That includes the crank, rods, pistons, and rings. It should cost around $200 because that engine is hard to balance. Balancing the engine will make it last longer and it will run smother. <P>If you were rebuilding your 2.8 with the original crankshaft then you could getaway with not balancing the engine on a stock rebuild.<P>I will tell you again that you will get more power out of a 3.4 with identical camshafts than a 3.2 and it would be cheaper.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||07-18-2000||07:22 PM||||You've recieved some good advice.  It sounds like you already bought pistons & rings but you should mic the cylinders to determine if they need to be bored (they should always be honed to facilitate break-in).  Make sure your shop uses a deck plate if you are going to rev it up (loads cylinder deck during machining).  Also, since you are replacing the crank check the alignment of the journal bores.  The caps should at least be surfaced and align honed possibly align bored.  These items will make the unit reliable under load/stress.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34||||reg||1
